92186045417 true] [17592186437104 1069 "q-140307" 13194139534822 17592186045417 true] [17592186437104 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534822 17592186045417 true] [17592186437104 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" 13194139534822 17592186045417 true] [17592186437104 1072 #uuid "a9a20426-c841-5b8d-947d-6ac99fc75818" 13194139534822 17592186045417 true] [17592186437104 1074 "Notice that $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A.$ So let's apply the diagonalization algorithm to $A.$\n\n**Step 1.** From the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-7\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=7.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda =-7.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-7)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 12 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 12 & 6 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+{R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -6 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=-6{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-6{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have two linearly independent eigenvectors\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda =7.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-7I & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -4\\end{aligned}]-7[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-15 & -6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 6 & -11\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking for nonzero solutions of $(A-7I)x=0,$ we obtain the eigenvector \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n**Step 3.** We construct the invertible matrix $P$ using our eigenvectors, as follows:\n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}-6 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139534822 17592186045417 true] [17592186437105 1068 #edb/ref 17592186437102 13194139534822 17592186045417 true] [17592186437105 1069 "q-97014" 13194139534822 17592186045417 true] [17592186437105 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 13194139534822 17592186045417 true] [17592186437105 1071 "$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}],\\,D=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -11\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices shown above. Given that $A=PD{P}^{-1},$ which of the following could be the matrix $P?$" 13194139534822 17592186045417 true] [17592186437105 1072 #uuid "f43706f1-2fe0-517f-8d0c-63c125c3a2af" 13194139534822 17592186045417 true] [17592186437105 1074 "Notice that $A=PD{P}^{-1}$ is a diagonalization of the matrix $A.$ So let's apply the diagonalization algorithm to $A.$\n\n**Step 1.** From the diagonal matrix $D,$ we deduce that the eigenvalues of $A$ are \n\n$\\,$ ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-1\\,$ and $\\,{\\lambda }_{3}=-11.$\n\n**Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $A.$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda =-1.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-1)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations with the augmented matrix $M$ which we reduce to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -6 & 6 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -3 & 0\\end{aligned}] &  & \\begin{aligned}{R}_{2}:={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\ {R}_{3}:={R}_{3}+(-1){R}_{1}\\end{aligned} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn the reduced matrix above, there is one pivot column (the $1$st one). Thus, ${x}_{2}$ and ${x}_{3}$ are free variables. From the first equation, we obtain ${x}_{1}=3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3}.$ Hence, the general solution is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3{x}_{2}-3{x}_{3} \\\\ {x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{3}\\end{aligned}]={x}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{x}_{3}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2},{x}_{3}\\in R.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have two linearly independent eigenvectors\n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\,$ and $\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- Consider $\\lambda =-11.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-11)I & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -7 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & -4\\end{aligned}]-(-11)[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}9 & 3 & -3 \\\\ 2 & 4 & 6 \\\\ -1 & 3 & 7\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking for nonzero solutions of $(A-(-11)I)x=0,$ we obtain the eigenvector \n\n$\\,$ ${v}_{3}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -2 \\